**Proof.**

Let $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$, and let $A=\angle BAC$. Since $D\in BA$ with $DB=a$ and $E\in CA$ with $CE=a$, we have
$$AD=c-a,\qquad AE=b-a,\qquad \angle DAE=A.$$

**Circumradius of $\triangle ADE$.** By the law of cosines in $\triangle ADE$,
$$DE^{2}=(c-a)^{2}+(b-a)^{2}-2(c-a)(b-a)\cos A.$$
We simplify $DE^{2}-a^{2}$:
$$\begin{aligned}
DE^{2}-a^{2}
&=b^{2}+c^{2}+a^{2}-2a(b+c)-2(c-a)(b-a)\cos A\\
&=\bigl(a^{2}+2bc\cos A\bigr)+a^{2}-2a(b+c)-2(c-a)(b-a)\cos A\\
&=2a^{2}-2a(b+c)+2\cos A\bigl[bc-(c-a)(b-a)\bigr]\\
&=2a^{2}-2a(b+c)+2a\cos A\,(b+c-a)\\
&=2a\bigl[-(b+c-a)+\cos A\,(b+c-a)\bigr]\\
&=-2a(b+c-a)(1-\cos A).
\end{aligned}$$
Hence
$$DE^{2}=a^{2}-2a(b+c-a)(1-\cos A).\tag{1}$$

Now use the half-angle identities $1-\cos A=2\sin^{2}\tfrac A2$ and $\sin^{2}A=4\sin^{2}\tfrac A2\cos^{2}\tfrac A2$, together with
$$4bc\cos^{2}\tfrac A2=2bc(1+\cos A)=2bc+(b^{2}+c^{2}-a^{2})=(b+c)^{2}-a^{2}=(b+c-a)(a+b+c).$$
Therefore
$$2a(b+c-a)(1-\cos A)=4a(b+c-a)\sin^{2}\tfrac A2=\frac{16abc\,\sin^{2}\tfrac A2\cos^{2}\tfrac A2}{a+b+c}=\frac{4abc\,\sin^{2}A}{a+b+c}.$$
Substituting into (1),
$$DE^{2}=a^{2}-\frac{4abc\,\sin^{2}A}{a+b+c}.\tag{2}$$

Since $\angle DAE=A$, the circumradius $R_{ADE}$ of $\triangle ADE$ satisfies
$$R_{ADE}=\frac{DE}{2\sin A},\qquad\text{so}\qquad R_{ADE}^{2}=\frac{DE^{2}}{4\sin^{2}A}= \frac{a^{2}}{4\sin^{2}A}-\frac{abc}{a+b+c}.\tag{3}$$

**Relating to $OI$.** Let $R$ and $r$ be the circumradius and inradius of $\triangle ABC$. Since $R=\dfrac{a}{2\sin A}$, the first term in (3) is $R^{2}$. For the second term, with $s=\dfrac{a+b+c}{2}$ and $\text{Area}=\dfrac12 bc\sin A$,
$$r=\frac{\text{Area}}{s}=\frac{bc\sin A}{a+b+c},\qquad 2Rr=2\cdot\frac{a}{2\sin A}\cdot\frac{bc\sin A}{a+b+c}=\frac{abc}{a+b+c}.$$
Thus (3) becomes
$$R_{ADE}^{2}=R^{2}-2Rr.$$

By Euler's formula for the distance between circumcenter and incenter,
$$OI^{2}=R^{2}-2Rr.$$
Hence $R_{ADE}^{2}=OI^{2}$, and since both quantities are positive,
$$R_{ADE}=OI. \qquad\blacksquare$$

